        <!-- FAILURE MODES (kept; the worked example is computed) -->
        <div class="dr-failure-modes" id="dr-fail">
          <div class="dr-failure-modes-label">How this strategy can fail</div>
          <p class="dr-failure-modes-intro">These rules assume price keeps returning toward the Power Law trend. The record shows that it has, which is not a guarantee that it will. The rules can fail in two ways.</p>
          <div class="dr-failure-mode">
            <div class="dr-failure-mode-title">1. Bitcoin leaves the channel.</div>
            <p>The Power Law has fit fifteen years of price, across nine orders of magnitude, with R&sup2; above 0.95. If price leaves the channel and stays out, the levels stop meaning anything.</p>
            <p><strong>If price stays below the floor,</strong> the sell rule never fires, and you take the whole fall with no cash set aside. <strong>If price stays above the upper band,</strong> the sell rule fires and the buy-back level never comes. You hold cash while bitcoin keeps rising, until the deadline buys back higher, or for good if there is no deadline. Neither rule can tell you which case you are in until well after the fact.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="dr-failure-mode">
            <div class="dr-failure-mode-title">2. The buy back costs more than the sale, and you end with less bitcoin than HODLing.</div>
            <p>Both rules can fire as designed, selling high against trend and buying back low against trend, and still leave you with less bitcoin than if you had held. It has happened, and the table above marks it &ldquo;Lost bitcoin&rdquo;.</p>
            <p id="dr2FailExample"></p>
            <p>The levels are multiples of the trend, but you sell and buy back in dollars. When the trend grows fast between the sale and the buy back, a price below trend can still be higher than the sale price. In 2013 the trend was growing about <span data-dr-math="g13">219%</span> a year; today it grows about <span data-dr-math="gNow">37%</span>, so the same failure now needs a much longer wait. It can still happen.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

