    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- TAB III: THE MATH — pedagogy and methodology -->
    <!-- v2 Stage A (2026-10-04): §1 and §4 rewritten, §5–§8 new; -->
    <!-- figures in spans with data-dr-math are filled live.       -->
    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="tab-content" id="tab-math">
        <div class="section-intro">
            <h2>The Math</h2>
            <p>The Calculator tab runs a standing rule through the actual bitcoin price record. This page walks through how the rule&rsquo;s levels are set, how a trigger fires, how a round trip changes the stack, how tax and lots work, why selling as a spike fades beat selling on the way up, what the buy-back deadline does, why 2013 hurts, and what isn&rsquo;t modelled. The goal is not to convince you the discipline is right for you; it&rsquo;s to make the calculator&rsquo;s reasoning auditable.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §1: LEVELS ARE MULTIPLES OF TREND -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>1. Levels are multiples of trend</h3>
            <p>The rule&rsquo;s levels are multiples of the Power Law trend: sell at 2&times; trend, buy back at 1&times;. A multiple is a fixed line. It means the same thing next year as it did in 2017.</p>
            <p>Each level shows a percentile as context: the share of the record since 2011 that sat at or above it, counted on the site&rsquo;s ~12-day samples. That is the modern set The Bitcoin Floor and How Much Cash use. Percentiles drift as data arrives. The 80th percentile is <span data-dr-math="p80all">1.75&times;</span> on the full record, <span data-dr-math="p80s11">1.78&times;</span> since 2011 and <span data-dr-math="p80s17">1.60&times;</span> since 2017, so a rule written as &ldquo;sell at the 80th percentile&rdquo; moves its own trigger. That is why the levels are multiples and the percentiles are context.</p>
            <div class="dr-math-table-wrap">
                <table class="dr-math-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Percentile</th><th>Full record</th><th>Since 2011</th><th>Since 2017</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody id="drMathPctBody"></tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="dr-math-note">Links shared before this version used percentiles. They still work: <code>?sell=80&amp;rebuy=50</code> lands on the since-2011 levels, about <span data-dr-math="p80s11">1.78&times;</span> and <span data-dr-math="p50s11">0.87&times;</span> trend.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §2: TRIGGER DETECTION -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>2. Trigger detection</h3>
            <p>The rule has two states: holding the stack, watching for a sale; and holding cash beside what&rsquo;s left, watching for the buy back.</p>
            <p>A <strong>sale</strong> fires one of two ways. <em>On the way up</em>, it fires when price crosses upward through the sell level: below it on one sample, at or above it on the next. <em>As the spike fades</em>, the rule arms when price reaches the sell level, then fires when price falls back below the fade level. The sale takes your chosen share of the bitcoin held at that moment.</p>
            <p>A <strong>buy back</strong> fires when price crosses downward through the buy-back level, or, if a deadline is set, at market once that many months have passed since the sale. It spends all the cash.</p>
            <ul class="dr-math-list">
                <li><strong>One open sale at a time.</strong> A sale does not re-fire while the rule holds cash, even if price crosses the level again. This stops oscillation around a level from generating trades.</li>
                <li><strong>Record ends mid-cycle.</strong> If the record ends while the rule holds cash, the table marks it &ldquo;still in cash&rdquo; and values the cash at the last price.</li>
                <li><strong>One event per sample.</strong> On a sample where the rule sells, it does not also buy back. The next sample can.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- §3: CYCLE ACCUMULATION (WORKED EXAMPLE) -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>3. Cycle accumulation &mdash; worked example</h3>
            <p>Each completed round trip changes your stack by the ratio of the two prices, applied to the share you sold:</p>
            <div class="dr-math-formula">
                <p>Per-cycle multiplier, before tax:</p>
                <code>new_stack = old_stack &times; (1 &minus; f + f &times; P_sell / P_rebuy)</code>
                <p class="dr-math-formula-caption">where <em>f</em> is the share sold, <em>P_sell</em> the sale price and <em>P_rebuy</em> the buy-back price.</p>
            </div>
            <p>The round trip grows the stack if and only if <em>P_sell</em> &gt; <em>P_rebuy</em>. Against trend this is always true (the sell level sits above the buy-back level). In dollars it is only sometimes true, which is the failure mode in <em>How this strategy can fail</em> on the Calculator tab.</p>
            <p>Sell at 1.75&times; trend and buy back at 0.85&times;, with the trend standing still: the sold share comes back <code>1.75 / 0.85 = 2.06&times;</code>. Selling half, the stack ends at <code>0.5 + 0.5 &times; 2.06 = 1.53&times;</code>. In practice the trend grows between the sale and the buy back, often for years, so the buy back costs more in dollars than that arithmetic suggests. The cycle table on the Calculator tab shows each real round trip, including the ones that ended below 1.00&times;.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §4: TAX AND LOTS -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>4. Tax, and which purchases are sold first</h3>
            <p>In an IRA or other tax-advantaged account, nothing is taxed. In a taxable account, each sale is taxed on its gain.</p>
            <p>The calculator tracks every purchase as a lot: the first bitcoin at the price on the start date, then each buy back at its own price. A sale draws on lots oldest first (FIFO), the default under US rules, or highest cost first, a form of specific identification that needs records. A lot held under a year is taxed at 32%, the federal ordinary rate, plus NIIT and state if set; otherwise at the long-term rate you choose (0%, 15% or 20%) plus NIIT and state. Tax is charged on the net gain across the lots sold, never below zero, and it comes out of the cash available to buy back. The previous version averaged the cost of all the bitcoin held. Average cost isn&rsquo;t an allowed method for bitcoin held directly.</p>
            <p class="dr-math-note">Source: IRS Rev. Proc. 2024-28 and the per-wallet cost-basis rules that apply from January 2025. Bitcoin held through spot ETFs is taxed as shares: check with your broker; most default to FIFO.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §5: SELL TIMING -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>5. Selling on the way up, or as the spike fades</h3>
            <p>A rule can sell as price climbs through a level, or wait for the spike to reach a level and sell when it turns back down. Waiting did better. Armed at 2&times; trend, selling half when price fell back below 2&times; and buying back at 0.85&times;, an IRA ended at <span data-dr-math="fade11">1.35&times;</span> HODL since 2011 and <span data-dr-math="fade14">2.25&times;</span> since 2014. Selling half on the way up through 1.75&times; ended at <span data-dr-math="up11">0.95&times;</span> and <span data-dr-math="up14">1.32&times;</span>. On the way up, the run-up kept going after the sale.</p>
            <p>The cost of waiting: a fade rule fires only if the spike reaches the level first. The 2024&ndash;25 spike peaked at <span data-dr-math="peak25">1.19&times;</span> trend, so no 2&times; rule fired. Bitcoin&rsquo;s Spikes found the same pattern for funding a bill: selling early on the way up was costly, selling as the spike rolled over was the practical option.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §6: THE DEADLINE -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>6. The buy-back deadline</h3>
            <p>A buy-back level set too deep can strand a sale in cash. Selling everything at 2&times; trend and waiting for 0.5&times; ended at <span data-dr-math="strand0">0.13&times;</span> HODL since 2011, because price never came back down far enough.</p>
            <p>A deadline buys back at market after a set number of months. Twelve months hurt: it tended to buy back in the middle of a bear market. Twenty-four months was neutral when the buy-back level came anyway, and it rescued the stranded cases: the same rule with a 24-month deadline ended at <span data-dr-math="strand24">0.61&times;</span>. That is why the presets use 24 months. With no deadline, the cycle table marks a sale still waiting as &ldquo;still in cash&rdquo;, valued at today&rsquo;s price.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §7: 2013 AND THE TREND'S GROWTH RATE -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>7. 2013, and how fast the trend grows</h3>
            <p>Most of the gap between the since-2011 and since-2014 results is one cycle. Rules that sold in March or April 2013, at about $70 to $131, bought back in January 2015 at about $234 to $270. Both trades were right against trend: high, then low. But the trend was then growing about <span data-dr-math="g13">219%</span> a year, so &ldquo;back below trend&rdquo; was a higher price than the sale.</p>
            <p>Today the trend grows about <span data-dr-math="gNow">37%</span> a year. The same failure now needs a much longer wait. &ldquo;Since 2014&rdquo; starts after that cycle, which is why the hero shows both, and it matches the record How Much Cash uses.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §8: SAMPLING -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>8. Sampling: a trigger fires on the first sample past its level</h3>
            <p>The record is the site&rsquo;s price series, one close about every 12 days. A rule fires on the first sample past its level, not at the level itself. In 2011 price went from below 1.5&times; trend to <span data-dr-math="samp11">2.94&times;</span> between two samples, so a rule that sells on the way up through 1.5&times; sold at <span data-dr-math="samp11">2.94&times;</span>. Daily closes would have sold lower.</p>
            <p>Every result on this page carries that sampling error. It was larger in the fast early years than it is now, when price moves less between samples.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- §9: HISTORICAL RECORD ≠ FORECAST -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>9. The historical record is not a forecast</h3>

            <p>The Calculator tab shows what the discipline would have done across actual bitcoin price history at the user's chosen settings. That is a fact about the past. It is not a prediction about the future. Three concrete ways the future will differ from the historical record:</p>

            <ul class="dr-math-list">
                <li><strong>Volatility compression.</strong> Bitcoin&rsquo;s cycle amplitudes have decreased over time. Cycle 1 peaked near 8&times; trend in 2011; recent cycles peak closer to 2&times; trend. The triggers that fired in 2011 or 2017 against extreme price excursions will likely fire less often in future cycles, even at the same levels. <a href="/the-power-law#theory">The Power Law Theory tab</a> documents this compression in detail.</li>
                <li><strong>Regime change is unmodeled.</strong> If the Power Law channel&rsquo;s shape shifts &mdash; if the slope changes, if mean reversion fails, if bitcoin breaks free of the channel and stays out &mdash; the levels become unreliable. The historical record does not exhibit this; future records might.</li>
                <li><strong>The strategy can fail without doing anything wrong.</strong> The historical record at certain settings shows cycles where both triggers fired correctly (high sell, low rebuy) and yet ended with less bitcoin than before, because the rebuy executed above the prior sell price. Detailed in the <em>How this strategy can fail</em> section on the Calculator tab.</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="dr-math-note">When a rule never fires across the record, the Calculator says so, and the discipline reduces to HODL across that span. None of the presets fired in the 2024&ndash;25 cycle. That is information about the framework, not a bug.</p>
        </div>

