        <!-- §11: WHAT I'M NOT MODELLING -->
        <div class="dr-math-section">
            <h3>11. What I&rsquo;m not modelling</h3>
            <p>The calculator focuses tightly on the cycle protocol. The following are deliberately out of scope:</p>

            <ul class="negative-space-list">
                <li><strong>Varying cash returns.</strong> Cash waiting to buy back earns 0% or a flat 4% a year. Real money-market rates ran from 0% to about 5% over the record.</li>
                <li><strong>Tax-loss harvesting.</strong> In a taxable account, capital losses elsewhere could offset cycle gains. The backtest doesn't model this; gains are taxed in isolation.</li>
                <li><strong>State short-term rates and thresholds.</strong> The state rate is each state&rsquo;s top long-term rate, applied flat. Washington is the exception, as two options: gains under about $270k a year (0%), and gains above it (7%), applied flat to every sale. Its 9.9% rate above about $1.27M a year, from 2025, isn&rsquo;t modelled.</li>
                <li><strong>Retirement-account contribution and withdrawal rules.</strong> Annual contribution limits, RMDs, Roth conversion mechanics, Backdoor / Mega-Backdoor pathways &mdash; none of this is modeled. The page is a cycle protocol calculator, not a retirement-account tax-strategy advisor.</li>
                <li><strong>Multi-account splits.</strong> A user with bitcoin partly in a retirement account and partly in a taxable account would benefit from running the discipline only on the retirement portion. This is a future page, not a feature of this one.</li>
                <li><strong>Future-cycle uncertainty.</strong> The historical record is one realization. A Monte Carlo treatment with stochastic volatility would surface a distribution of possible outcomes rather than a single line through the past &mdash; but it would also hide the fact that the past is the actual evidence. I treat the historical record as the primary thing worth showing, and the volatility-compression note above as the acknowledgment that future cycles will look different.</li>
                <li><strong>Behavioral cost.</strong> The discipline requires action at exactly the moments most users find hardest &mdash; selling into euphoria, rebuying into fear. The calculator assumes perfect execution of the protocol.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- §12: METHODOLOGY DISCLOSURE FOOTER -->
        <div class="dr-math-disclosure">
            <p><strong>Methodology disclosure</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Power Law coefficients used: <code>a = 1.6 &times; 10&minus;&sup1;&#8311;</code>, <code>b = 5.77</code> (reference Porkopolis values &mdash; see <a href="/the-power-law#channel">The Channel</a> for attribution)</li>
                <li><code>PL_DATA</code>: the site&rsquo;s ~12-day price samples from mid-2010 to the present</li>
                <li>Percentiles: share of samples since January 2011 (also shown for the full record and since 2017)</li>
                <li>Backtest: one pass through <code>PL_DATA</code> from the first sample of the start month (2011, 2014 or 2017), 1 BTC at that sample&rsquo;s price; crossing triggers, one open sale at a time</li>
                <li>Cash returns: 0% or 4% a year, compounded between samples</li>
                <li>Tax: in a taxable account, net gain on the lots sold (FIFO or highest cost first); 32% plus NIIT and state on lots held under a year, the chosen long-term rate plus NIIT and state otherwise</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
